Transient functional dyspepsia and fullness post-consumption of rich or fatty foods
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Postprandial gastric fullness, Epigastric bloating, Fatty food trigger

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-044
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe epigastric discomfort and bloating requiring assessment to rule out biliary colic, acute pancreatitis, or peptic ulcer perforation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted sudden, uncomfortable epigastric fullness and bloating following a meal. Urgent clinical evaluation was necessary prior to the next in-hours period to palpate the abdomen, assess for peritoneal signs, and exclude acute surgical emergencies such as cholecystitis or early pancreatitis which present with similar postprandial discomfort.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I feel incredibly bloated and uncomfortably full right under my ribs after eating that heavy dinner.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes an uncomfortable sensation of postprandial gastric fullness and mild epigastric bloating occurring shortly after a meal. Reports frequent spontaneous belching which provides temporary relief. A clear relationship to ingesting high-fat content meals is noted. Complete absence of distinct gnawing pain waking the patient from sleep. Lack of dysphagia, odynophagia, or severe nausea. Normal appetite outside the acute postprandial window. No history of hematemesis or melena.
Functional Status: Fully independent, uncomfortable when sitting forward.
Social/Safety Concerns: Discomfort is causing anxiety regarding heart attack or serious stomach disease.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 126/82 | HR: 76 | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 84 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, frequently shifting position to find comfort.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals a soft, non-distended epigastric region. The epigastrium is non-tender to deep palpation. Diagnostic triad confirmed: postprandial gastric fullness, epigastric bloating, and a fatty food trigger. Normal active bowel sounds. Negative Murphy's sign. No palpable masses or organomegaly. No guarding or rebound tenderness. Cardiovascular exam is normal with clear heart sounds and no radiating chest pain.
Environmental Observations: Remnants of a heavy, fried meal visible.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Intermittent use of over-the-counter antacids.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Transient functional dyspepsia and fullness post-consumption of rich or fatty foods - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Biliary Colic/Cholecystitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of focal right upper quadrant pain, negative Murphy's sign, and lack of radiation to the right scapula.

Peptic Ulcer Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of gnawing, burning pain relieved by food, and soft non-tender epigastrium.

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal cardiovascular exam, absence of exertional chest pressure, and clear correlation to a heavy meal without autonomic signs.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The acute onset of significant epigastric discomfort following a meal shares a presentation with early biliary disease, acute pancreatitis, and atypical myocardial infarction. An urgent physical examination was mandated to perform a thorough abdominal and cardiovascular assessment. Confirming the absence of peritoneal signs, biliary tenderness, and cardiac symptoms safely ruled out life-threatening pathology. This allowed the condition to be confidently managed as benign dietary indiscretion, preventing an unnecessary ED transfer.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance regarding the benign abdominal exam. Recommendation for an over-the-counter liquid antacid/simethicone preparation for immediate symptomatic relief.
Positive Behavioral Change: Eat slowly and stop eating when feeling 80% full, rather than completely full, allowing the delayed stretch receptors in the stomach time to signal the brain before severe overdistension occurs.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review if symptoms become a daily occurrence or if unintentional weight loss occurs.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you develop severe, sharp, unrelenting abdominal or chest pain, pain radiating to your back or shoulder, or if you begin vomiting dark material resembling coffee grounds.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical functional dyspepsia; recurrent severe episodes may warrant an ultrasound to definitively exclude gallstones.
Clinical Course: The bloating and fullness will gradually resolve over 4 to 6 hours as the heavy meal empties from the stomach.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you develop a high fever, if your skin or eyes turn yellow, or if you develop difficulty swallowing.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: High-fat meals empty from the stomach very slowly. This causes the stomach to remain distended for hours, leading to a sensation of severe stretching, bloating, and reflex belching. The examination shows no signs of gallstones or infection.
Actionable Steps: Avoid lying completely flat; stay semi-upright to aid digestion. Take a short, gentle walk. Avoid eating large, fatty meals late at night.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Indigestion/Bloating from a heavy meal. Stomach is soft and safe. Take an over-the-counter antacid. Stay upright. Watch for severe chest or back pain.

SMS EDUCATION

